theory.intro:
The special quadrilaterals are nested inside one another, not separate boxes. A shape keeps **every** name whose properties it satisfies, so one shape can have several correct names. Settle a claim by checking the defining properties, not by how the shape looks.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\node[draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt] (Q) at (0,6.6) {Quadrilateral};
\node[draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt] (K) at (-4.2,4.6) {Kite};
\node[draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt] (P) at (2.2,4.6) {Parallelogram};
\node[draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt] (Rh) at (-2,2.4) {Rhombus};
\node[draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt] (Re) at (4.4,2.4) {Rectangle};
\node[draw, rounded corners, inner sep=4pt] (Sq) at (1.2,0.4) {Square};
\draw[->] (Q) -- (K);
\draw[->] (Q) -- (P);
\draw[->] (K) -- (Rh);
\draw[->] (P) -- (Rh);
\draw[->] (P) -- (Re);
\draw[->] (Rh) -- (Sq);
\draw[->] (Re) -- (Sq);
\node[font=\small] at (0.4,-1) {Each arrow points to a more specific type.};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

